---
import { docsUrl } from '../lib/site';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'How Atlas works — From connected APIs to safer change';
const description = 'See how Atlas turns an integration into a reviewed workflow, runs it on Temporal, and helps your team respond when an API changes.';
const steps = [
  ['01', 'Describe the work', 'Connect machine-readable API contracts and approve the operations your team can use. Describe the integration in plain English. Atlas drafts the steps and data mappings from those approved operations.'],
  ['02', 'Review before it runs', 'Atlas validates the workflow and generates tests for review. A person approves the exact workflow version and API versions that may run. Review the retry rules and how repeated requests affect each write.'],
  ['03', 'Run with recovery built in', 'A worker executes the approved steps using Temporal. Temporal preserves workflow progress across process failures and manages retries according to the workflow rules. Operators can inspect run state and use the available repair controls.'],
  ['04', 'Keep changes reviewable', 'When Atlas rediscovers a connected contract, it identifies known workflow steps affected by the change. Breaking changes pause new intake. A proposed replacement passes through validation, tests, and approval before activation.'],
];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} pathname="/how-it-works/">
  <SiteHeader current="how" />
  <main id="main">
    <header class="page-hero shell"><p class="eyebrow"><span aria-hidden="true"></span> How it works</p><h1>Understand the systems.<br />Own the next step.</h1><p class="lede">Connect the API knowledge to the work it powers. Atlas brings workflow creation, approval, execution, and change review into one process your team can operate.</p><a class="text-link" href="#reference-workflow">See the Payment and Billing example →</a></header>
    <section class="section shell process" aria-label="The workflow lifecycle"><div class="process-grid">{steps.map(([number, heading, body]) => <article><span class="phase-mark" aria-hidden="true">{number}</span><div><h2>{heading}</h2><p>{body}</p></div></article>)}</div></section>
    <section class="section reference shell" id="reference-workflow" aria-labelledby="reference-title">
      <div class="reference-intro"><p class="kicker">Local demo · Payment and Billing</p><h2 id="reference-title">Follow a payment all the way through.</h2><p>A payment arrives. The workflow reads the payment and invoice, checks amount and currency, settles the invoice, publishes an event, and notifies operations. The demo uses local mock APIs so the team can rehearse failure and recovery.</p></div>
      <div class="reference-flow" aria-label="Payment workflow sequence"><div><span>Read</span><strong>Payment received</strong><small>Start with a payment ID</small></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><span>Verify</span><strong>Payment + invoice</strong><small>Check amount and currency</small></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><span>Settle</span><strong>Update Billing</strong><small>Apply duplicate-handling rules</small></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><span>Complete</span><strong>Notify operations</strong><small>Inspect the outcome</small></div></div>
      <div class="drift-path" id="change"><p class="kicker">Now remove a field from the Billing contract</p><div class="drift-grid"><div><strong>Detect</strong><p>Rediscover the API contract and identify the removed field.</p></div><div><strong>Locate</strong><p>Find the affected step in the workflows Atlas manages.</p></div><div><strong>Pause</strong><p>Hold new intake for review. Existing runs retain their approved versions.</p></div><div><strong>Approve</strong><p>Test and review the replacement before activating it.</p></div></div></div>
      <p class="term">This demonstrates the local implementation. Detection depends on rediscovering connected contracts; changes outside those contracts need separate monitoring.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section shell" id="mulesoft" aria-labelledby="migration-title"><header class="section-heading"><div><p class="kicker">Evaluating a MuleSoft replacement</p><h2 id="migration-title">Prove one flow before you move traffic.</h2></div><p>Choose a contained API workflow with a known owner, test data, and a clear business result. Agree on success before rebuilding it.</p></header><div class="value-grid"><article><h3>1. Check the fit</h3><p>Inventory its APIs, transformations, authentication, schedules, and side effects. Check which Atlas operations are supported and what requires additional work.</p></article><article><h3>2. Rehearse the hard parts</h3><p>Compare outputs using test data. Repeat requests, interrupt a worker, and simulate a provider outage. Avoid sending duplicate writes to real systems during comparison.</p></article><article><h3>3. Measure and cut over</h3><p>Compare build time, recovery effort, and operating costs. Define the traffic switch, handling of in-flight work, and rollback plan before retiring the original flow.</p></article></div></section>
    <aside class="maturity shell"><p class="kicker">Developer preview</p><div><h2>Start with a local evaluation.</h2><p>Evaluate the workflow runtime through the developer setup. A separate customer web and company sign-in package is implemented; the complete customer runtime package is still in development. Check API coverage and operating requirements before rollout.</p><p><a class="text-link" href={docsUrl('/self-host/')}>Read the self-hosting guide →</a></p></div></aside><section class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">From example to evidence</p><h2>Now bring your own workflow.</h2><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/evaluate/">Build an evaluation brief <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="/compare/">Compare approaches</a></div></section>
  </main><SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
